From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 13:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] shared/tinyusb: stall the CDC IN endpoint before clearing its
 TX FIFO on disconnect.

On an ESP32-S3 on its native USB, a machine.USBDevice disconnect while
the board is still sending over the built-in CDC rebooted it ("Guru
Meditation Error: Core 1 panic'ed (Interrupt wdt timeout on CPU1)")
(micropython-pydevices#70). Both active(False) and a switch from the
built-in device to a runtime one (active(True) while connected) did it.

mp_usbd_disconnect() cleared the CDC TX FIFO and then stalled the CDC IN
endpoint. With TinyUSB 0.21 (patch 0014) the DWC2 driver runs in slave
mode and pulls each packet of a CDC transfer straight from that FIFO in
its TX-FIFO-empty interrupt, so this is the same failure patch 0024 fixed
in the line-state callback: emptied mid-transfer, the interrupt writes
nothing, stays asserted and re-enters until the watchdog fires. A coredump
from the unpatched build shows it interrupting edpt_disable() under
usbd_edpt_stall(), called from mp_usbd_disconnect().

So the stall now comes first. Disabling the endpoint masks its
TX-FIFO-empty interrupt (patches/tinyusb/0001), so by the time the clear
runs nothing pulls from the FIFO any more.

The disconnect also hung the board, with no REPL on either port: the
endpoint disable polled interrupt flags the USB interrupt handler can
clear. That is fixed in TinyUSB itself by patches/tinyusb/0001.

Proven on a Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-7 (ESP32_GENERIC_S3 LCD_7,
native USB CDC on Windows), driven from its UART console while a host
reads the CDC port. Each trial checks the host is receiving, prints 1000
characters and disconnects at once or 50 to 300 us later, then counts
resets and hangs on the UART. Before this patch, active(False) mid-print
failed 13 times in 40 (8 panics, 5 hangs) and the switch to a runtime
device 7 times in 20 (4 panics, 3 hangs).

With patches/tinyusb/0001 the endpoint disable no longer hangs, and most
of the panics went with it, since the interrupt now stops pulling as soon
as the stall begins. The clear still comes first without this patch,
though, and an interrupt landing between the two calls still panicked the
board once in 240 trials of both kinds. With this patch the two kinds
passed 0 of 89 and 0 of 86.

The ESP32-S2 and ESP32-P4 build TinyUSB 0.21 the same way, in slave mode
(CFG_TUD_DWC2_DMA_ENABLE defaults to 0), so they share the path.

Drop this patch when upstream reorders the disconnect or TinyUSB makes
tud_cdc_n_write_clear() safe under an in-flight transfer.

Based-on: micropython 0fd6c573e (tag v1.29.0)
---
 shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
--- a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
+++ b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
@@ -484,10 +484,17 @@
     }
 
     #if MICROPY_HW_USB_CDC
-    // Ensure no pending static CDC writes, as these can cause TinyUSB to crash
-    tud_cdc_write_clear();
     // Prevent cdc write flush from initiating any new transfers while disconnecting
+    //
+    // This comes before the clear below. In slave mode the DWC2 driver
+    // (ESP32-S2/S3/P4) pulls each packet of an IN transfer straight from the
+    // CDC TX FIFO in its interrupt: emptied mid-transfer, the TX-FIFO-empty
+    // interrupt finds nothing to write, stays asserted, and re-enters until
+    // the interrupt watchdog resets the chip. The stall disables the endpoint
+    // and masks that interrupt first.
     usbd_edpt_stall(RHPORT, USBD_CDC_EP_IN);
+    // Ensure no pending static CDC writes, as these can cause TinyUSB to crash.
+    tud_cdc_write_clear();
     #endif
 
     bool was_connected = tud_connected();
